
  - [1mIn-app feed[0m (bell icon, unread count, list) — no external services, no permissions, no deliverability. Fully
    testable offline. But it only works while someone has the app open; it will never pull anyone back.
  - [1mReal-time in-app[0m (SSE or WebSocket pushing into that feed) — the feed updates without a refresh. Moderate cost: a
    long-lived connection, reconnect logic, and a deploy target that tolerates it. Pure enhancement over the feed,
    addable any time.
  - [1mEmail[0m — the only channel that reaches someone who isn't looking. Costs an email provider, templates, bounce
    handling, and — this is the real cost — [1mdigesting and rate limits[0m, because an un-digested task app will email
    someone eleven times in a morning and they will mute you permanently.
  - [1mWeb push[0m — reaches a closed tab. Needs a service worker, VAPID keys, HTTPS, and a permission prompt users mostly
    decline. Lowest value per unit of work at this stage.

  Cheap to change later: adding channels, [3mif[0m the notification record and the delivery step are separate from the start.
  Expensive later: retrofitting per-user channel preferences and digest batching onto a system that assumed one channel
  — that reshapes the schema and the send path.

  [1mMy recommendation: build the in-app feed first[0m, behind a delivery interface with a [38;5;153mnotification[39m record that doesn't
  know how it's sent. Then email as the second channel once we can see what the real event volume looks like. Web push
  I'd defer indefinitely.
